  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the fundamental relationship of traffic flow?",
      "a": "Flow equals density times speed: q equals k times v. Thirty vehicles per kilometre moving at 63 kilometres per hour gives 1,890 vehicles per hour.",
      "asks": [
        "q = kv",
        "Relationship between speed flow and density"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is Greenshields' model?",
      "a": "Greenshields' 1935 model says speed falls in a straight line with density, from the free-flow speed on an empty road to zero at jam density. It makes the flow-density curve a parabola.",
      "asks": [
        "Greenshields traffic model"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is road capacity?",
      "a": "Capacity is the maximum flow a lane can carry. In the Greenshields model it is free speed times jam density over 4; with 90 kilometres per hour and 100 vehicles per kilometre that is 2,250 vehicles per hour.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you find the capacity of a lane?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is jam density?",
      "a": "Jam density is the density at which traffic stops completely, typically around 100 to 150 vehicles per kilometre per lane, depending on vehicle length and gaps.",
      "asks": [
        "Define jam density"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do phantom traffic jams form?",
      "a": "Near capacity, one driver braking makes the next brake harder, and the wave grows and travels backwards. Flow drops even though nothing blocks the road.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a shockwave in traffic?",
        "Traffic jam for no reason"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Bruce Greenshields published his linear speed–density model in 1935.",
    "In a 2008 experiment led by Yuki Sugiyama, 22 cars on a 230 m circular track formed a jam with no bottleneck."
